// # Storage Strategies
// 
// Comprehensive documentation for data persistence strategies in standalone mode,
// covering browser storage, cloud storage, and file system access.
// 
// ## 📑 Table of Contents
// 
// - [Overview](#overview)
// - [Storage Architecture](#storage-architecture)
// - [Storage Options](#storage-options)
// - [Storage Guides](#storage-guides)
// - [Conflict Resolution](#conflict-resolution)
// - [Best Practices](#best-practices)
// 
// ## Overview
// 
// Standalone mode requires comprehensive data persistence strategies to replace
// Mountain's file system with web-native storage solutions.
// 
// ### Storage Architecture
// 
// ```mermaid
// graph TB
//     subgraph "Browser Storage Layer"
//         IDB[IndexedDB<br/>Large Data]
//         LS[LocalStorage<br/>Settings]
//         SS[SessionStorage<br/>Session]
//         CACHE[Cache API<br/>Assets]
//     end
// 
//     subgraph "Storage Abstraction Layer"
//         VFS[Virtual File System]
//         CONFIG[Configuration Store]
//         CACHE_MGR[Cache Manager]
//         SYNC[Sync Manager]
//     end
// 
//     subgraph "Cloud Storage Layer"
//         FIREBASE[Firebase<br/>Firestore]
//         SUPABASE[Supabase<br/>PostgreSQL]
//         AWS[AWS S3/R2]
//         GCS[Google Cloud Storage]
//     end
// 
//     subgraph "File System Access"
//         FSA[File System Access API<br/>Native Files]
//     end
// 
//     subgraph "Application Layer"
//         EDITOR[Editor]
//         TERMINAL[Terminal]
//         WORKSPACE[Workspace]
//         COLLAB[Collaboration]
//     end
// 
//     EDITOR --> VFS
//     TERMINAL --> CONFIG
//     WORKSPACE --> SYNC
//     COLLAB --> SYNC
// 
//     VFS --> IDB
//     VFS --> FSA
//     VFS <--> SYNC
// 
//     CONFIG --> LS
//     CONFIG --> CACHE
// 
//     CACHE_MGR --> CACHE
//     CACHE_MGR --> CACHE_API
// 
//     SYNC --> FIREBASE
//     SYNC --> SUPABASE
//     SYNC --> AWS
//     SYNC --> GCS
// 
//     FSA <--> OS[Operating System]
// ```
// 
// ### Storage Decision Matrix
// 
// | Use Case            | Primary Storage        | Secondary Storage | When to Use                      |
// | ------------------- | ---------------------- | ----------------- | -------------------------------- |
// | **Editor Content**  | IndexedDB              | Cloud Sync        | Large files, frequent writes     |
// | **User Settings**   | LocalStorage           | Cloud Sync        | Small data, infrequent changes   |
// | **Session State**   | SessionStorage         | -                 | Temporary data, cleared on close |
// | **Asset Caching**   | Cache API              | CDN               | Images, fonts, static resources  |
// | **File System**     | File System Access API | IndexedDB         | User gesture required            |
// | **Collab State**    | Cloud Storage          | IndexedDB         | Real-time sync needed            |
// | **Build Artifacts** | Cloud Storage          | IndexedDB         | Shareable, large files           |
// 
// ## Storage Options
// 
// ### 1. Browser Storage
// 
// #### IndexedDB
// 
// **Strengths:**
// 
// - Large storage capacity (hundreds of MB to GB)
// - Asynchronous, non-blocking
// - Stores complex objects and blobs
// - Persistent across sessions
// 
// **Weaknesses:**
// 
// - Complex API
// - Requires careful schema design
// - Not suitable for small, frequent writes
// 
// **Best For:** File contents, large datasets, offline storage
// 
// ```typescript
// // IndexedDB Schema Design
// interface IDBSchema {
// 	files: {
// 		key: string;
// 		value: {
// 			path: string;
// 			content: string;
// 			language: string;
// 			modified: number;
// 			created: number;
// 		};
// 		indexes: {
// 			"by-path": string;
// 			"by-modified": number;
// 		};
// 	};
// 	projects: {
// 		key: string;
// 		value: {
// 			id: string;
// 			name: string;
// 			description: string;
// 			files: string[];
// 			settings: ProjectSettings;
// 		};
// 	};
// 	settings: {
// 		key: string;
// 		value: {
// 			key: string;
// 			value: unknown;
// 			modified: number;
// 		};
// 	};
// }
// ```
// 
// #### LocalStorage
// 
// **Strengths:**
// 
// - Simple API
// - Synchronous access
// - Persistent across sessions
// - Wide browser support
// 
// **Weaknesses:**
// 
// - Limited capacity (~5-10MB)
// - Synchronous (blocks UI on large writes)
// - String-only storage
// - Not suitable for large data
// 
// **Best For:** User preferences, theme settings, small configuration
// 
// #### SessionStorage
// 
// **Strengths:**
// 
// - Simple API
// - Cleared on tab close
// - Synchronous access
// 
// **Weaknesses:**
// 
// - Limited capacity
// - Not persistent
// - Tab-scoped only
// 
// **Best For:** Temporary state, form data, navigation state
// 
// #### Cache API
// 
// **Strengths:**
// 
// - Designed for HTTP requests
// - Supports service workers
// - Good for offline functionality
// - Can store any Response
// 
// **Weaknesses:**
// 
// - Request/Response pairs only
// - Requires Service Worker
// - Manual cache management
// 
// **Best For:** API responses, static assets, offline caching
// 
// ### 2. Cloud Storage
// 
// #### Firebase
// 
// **Strengths:**
// 
// - Real-time sync
// - Built-in authentication
// - Offline support
// - Easy integration
// 
// **Weaknesses:**
// 
// - Pricing can be complex
// - Vendor lock-in
// - Rate limits on free tier
// 
// **Best For:** Collaboration, real-time features, small to medium projects
// 
// **See:** [`cloud-storage.md`](./cloud-storage.md#firebase)
// 
// #### Supabase
// 
// **Strengths:**
// 
// - PostgreSQL-based
// - Real-time subscriptions
// - Open source
// - SQL-based queries
// 
// **Weaknesses:**
// 
// - Less mature than Firebase
// - Requires SQL knowledge
// - Pricing for larger projects
// 
// **Best For:** SQL-based apps, complex queries, open source projects
// 
// **See:** [`cloud-storage.md`](./cloud-storage.md#supabase)
// 
// #### AWS S3 / Cloudflare R2
// 
// **Strengths:**
// 
// - Highly scalable
// - Cost-effective
// - CDN integration
// - Wide range of tools
// 
// **Weaknesses:**
// 
// - Not real-time
// - Requires orchestration for sync
// - Learning curve
// 
// **Best For:** File storage, large assets, high-traffic apps
// 
// **See:** [`cloud-storage.md`](./cloud-storage.md#aws)
// 
// ### 3. File System Access API
// 
// **Strengths:**
// 
// - Native file system access
// - User permission-based
// - Large file support
// - Familiar UX
// 
// **Weaknesses:**
// 
// - Requires user gestures
// - Permission resets on navigation
// - Chrome/Edge only (Firefox limited)
// - HTTPS required
// 
// **Best For:** Desktop web apps, file-based workflows, power users
// 
// **See:** [`local-file-system.md`](./local-file-system.md)
// 
// ## Storage Guides
// 
// ### Browser Storage
// 
// [`browser-storage.md`](./browser-storage.md) - Complete guide for browser
// storage:
// 
// - IndexedDB schema design
// - IndexedDB wrapper utilities
// - LocalStorage/SessionStorage patterns
// - Cache API usage
// - Quota management
// 
// ### Cloud Storage
// 
// [`cloud-storage.md`](./cloud-storage.md) - Complete guide for cloud storage:
// 
// - Firebase integration
// - Supabase integration
// - AWS S3 integration
// - Cloudflare R2 integration
// - Conflict resolution strategies
// 
// ### File System Access
// 
// [`local-file-system.md`](./local-file-system.md) - Complete guide for File
// System Access API:
// 
// - File system access patterns
// - Permission handling
// - File watcher implementation
// - Sync with browser storage
