// # Common Transport Abstractions
// 
// This directory contains the unified abstractions and patterns that work across
// all transport types (gRPC, IPC, WASM) in CodeEditorLand.
// 
// ## 📑 Table of Contents
// 
// - [Overview](#overview)
// - [Architecture](#architecture)
// - [Core Documents](#core-documents)
// - [Transport Interface](#transport-interface)
// - [Cross-Language Support](#cross-language-support)
// - [Unified Patterns](#unified-patterns)
// - [Implementation Guidelines](#implementation-guidelines)
// 
// ## Overview
// 
// The common transport abstractions provide a unified, transport-agnostic
// interface that enables all CodeEditorLand components (Cocoon, Grove, Mountain,
// Air, Vine, Wind, Sky) to communicate seamlessly regardless of the underlying
// transport mechanism.
// 
// ### Design Philosophy
// 
// | Principle              | Description                                         | Benefit                      |
// | ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
// | **Transport Agnostic** | Components don't know which transport they're using | Easy to swap transports      |
// | **Type Safe**          | Strong typing in Rust and TypeScript                | Compile-time error detection |
// | **Async First**        | All operations are asynchronous                     | Non-blocking I/O             |
// | **Observable**         | Built-in metrics and logging                        | Production-ready monitoring  |
// | **Extensible**         | Easy to add new transports                          | Future-proof                 |
// 
// ### Transport Types Supported
// 
// | Transport | Protocol            | Use Case                       | Status     |
// | --------- | ------------------- | ------------------------------ | ---------- |
// | **gRPC**  | HTTP/2 + Protobuf   | Cross-process, remote          | ✅ Active  |
// | **IPC**   | Native IPC          | Same-process, high performance | 🔄 Planned |
// | **WASM**  | WebSocket/WebWorker | Browser-based applications     | 🔄 Planned |
// 
// ## Architecture
// 
// ### Layer Diagram
// 
// ```mermaid
// graph TB
//     A[Application Layer] --> B[Transport Abstraction]
//     B --> C[gRPC Transport]
//     B --> D[IPC Transport]
//     B --> E[WASM Transport]
// 
//     C --> F[Network]
//     D --> G[OS Kernel]
//     E --> H[Browser]
// 
//     style B fill:#48dbfb
//     style C fill:#1dd1a1
//     style D fill:#feca57
//     style E fill:#ff6b6b
// ```
// 
// ### Component Integration
// 
// ```mermaid
// graph LR
//     A[Cocoon - TS] --> B[Transport]
//     C[Mountain - Rust] --> B
//     D[Grove - Rust/WASM] --> B
//     E[Wind - TS] --> B
//     F[Sky - TS] --> B
// 
//     B --> G[gRPC]
//     B --> H[IPC]
//     B --> I[WASM]
// 
//     style B fill:#48dbfb
// ```
// 
// ## Core Documents
// 
// | Document                                       | Description                                              |
// | ---------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
// | [`request-response.md`](./request-response.md) | Unified request/response model for all transports        |
// | [`error-handling.md`](./error-handling.md)     | Error taxonomy, codes, retry strategies, circuit breaker |
// | [`serialization.md`](./serialization.md)       | Data serialization format comparison and guidelines      |
// | [`metrics.md`](./metrics.md)                   | Comprehensive metrics and monitoring strategy            |
// 
// ## Transport Interface
// 
// ### Unified Core Trait
// 
// All transports implement the same core interface, enabling seamless switching.
// 
// #### Rust Interface
// 
// ```rust
// #[async_trait]
// pub trait TransportStrategy {
//     type Request;
//     type Response;
//     type Error;
// 
//     // Connection management
//     async fn connect(&mut self) -> Result<(), Self::Error>;
//     async fn disconnect(&mut self) -> Result<(), Self::Error>;
// 
//     // Communication
//     async fn send_request(&mut self, request: Self::Request) -> Result<Self::Response, Self::Error>;
//     async fn send_notification(&mut self, notification: Notification) -> Result<(), Self::Error>;
//     async fn stream_events(&mut self) -> Result<Stream<Event>, Self::Error>;
// 
//     // Status
//     fn is_connected(&self) -> bool;
//     fn latency_ms(&self) -> u64;
// }
// ```
// 
// #### TypeScript Interface
// 
// ```typescript
// interface TransportStrategy<TRequest, TResponse> {
// 	// Connection management
// 	connect(): Promise<void>;
// 	disconnect(): Promise<void>;
// 
// 	// Communication
// 	sendRequest(request: TRequest): Promise<TResponse>;
// 	sendNotification(notification: Notification): Promise<void>;
// 	streamEvents(): AsyncIterable<Event>;
// 
// 	// Status
// 	isConnected: boolean;
// 	latencyMs: number;
// }
// ```
// 
// #### WASM Interface
// 
// ```typescript
// interface WasmTransport {
// 	connect(): number;
// 	sendRequest(requestPtr: number, requestLen: number): number;
// 	readResponse(ptr: number, len: number): number;
// 	disconnect(): void;
// 	isConnected(): number;
// 	getLatencyMs(): number;
// }
// ```
// 
// ## Cross-Language Support
// 
// ### Language Compatibility Matrix
// 
// | Feature             | Rust              | TypeScript       | WASM           | Go                 | Java                 |
// | ------------------- | ----------------- | ---------------- | -------------- | ------------------ | -------------------- |
// | **Transport Trait** | ✅ Native trait   | ✅ Interface     | ✅ Exports     | ✅ Interface       | ✅ Interface         |
// | **Async/Await**     | ✅ Future         | ✅ Promise       | ✅ Promise     | ✅ Goroutine       | ✅ CompletableFuture |
// | **Error Handling**  | ✅ Result type    | ✅ Try/catch     | ✅ Error codes | ✅ Error interface | ✅ Exceptions        |
// | **Serialization**   | ✅ Prost          | ✅ protobufjs    | ✅ Custom      | ✅ Protobuf        | ✅ Protobuf          |
// | **Metrics**         | ✅ Prometheus-rs  | ✅ prom-client   | ✅ Custom      | ✅ Prometheus      | ✅ Micrometer        |
// | **Streaming**       | ✅ Futures Stream | ✅ AsyncIterable | ✅ Stream      | ✅ Channel         | ✅ Flow              |
// 
// ### Data Types Mapping
// 
// | Concept            | Rust                      | TypeScript            | Description               |
// | ------------------ | ------------------------- | --------------------- | ------------------------- | ----------------------- |
// | **Correlation ID** | `u64`                     | `BigInt`              | Request/response matching |
// | **Timestamp**      | `i64`                     | `BigInt               | number`                   | Nanoseconds since epoch |
// | **Duration**       | `Duration`                | `number` (ms)         | Timeout duration          |
// | **Binary Data**    | `Vec<u8>`                 | `Uint8Array`          | Binary payload            |
// | **Metadata**       | `HashMap<String, String>` | `Map<string, string>` | Key-value pairs           |
// 
// ### Protocol Buffer Compatibility
// 
// ```protobuf
// // Shared schema: transport/v1/common.proto
// message UnifiedRequest {
//     uint64 correlation_id = 1;
//     string method = 2;
//     bytes payload = 3;
//     map<string, string> metadata = 4;
//     int64 timestamp = 5;
//     uint32 timeout_ms = 6;
//     uint32 priority = 7;
// }
// ```
// 
// **Generated Code:**
// 
// - **Rust:** Use `prost` generator
// - **TypeScript:** Use `ts-proto` or `protobufjs`
// - **WASM:** Custom bridging layer
// 
// ## Unified Patterns
// 
// ### Request Lifecycle
// 
// All transports follow the same request lifecycle:
// 
// ```mermaid
// stateDiagram-v2
//     [*] --> Pending: Create Request
//     Pending --> Sent: send_request()
//     Sent --> Responding: Response Received
//     Responding --> Completed: Success
//     Responding --> Failed: Error
//     Sent --> Timeout: Timeout Elapsed
//     Completed --> [*]
//     Failed --> [*]
//     Timeout --> [*]
// ```
// 
// ### Error Handling Strategy
// 
// Unified error taxonomy across all transports:
// 
// ```mermaid
// graph TD
//     A[Transport Errors] --> A1[Connection]
//     A --> A2[Timeout]
//     B[Protocol Errors] --> B1[Serialization]
//     B --> B2[Validation]
//     C[Application Errors] --> C1[Permission]
//     C --> C2[Rate Limit]
// 
//     style A fill:#ff6b6b
//     style B fill:#feca57
//     style C fill:#48dbfb
// ```
// 
// ### Retry Strategies
// 
// Consistent retry behavior across transports:
// 
// | Error Type             | Strategy                | Max Attempts | Backoff                          |
// | ---------------------- | ----------------------- | ------------ | -------------------------------- |
// | **Connection Failed**  | Exponential             | 5            | 100ms, 200ms, 400ms, 800ms, 1.6s |
// | **Timeout**            | Exponential with Jitter | 3            | 200ms, 400ms, 800ms              |
// | **Rate Limited (429)** | Fixed Delay             | 5            | 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s, 16s              |
// | **Server Error (5xx)** | Linear                  | 3            | 500ms, 1s, 1.5s                  |
// | **Validation Error**   | No Retry                | 0            | N/A                              |
// 
// ### Circuit Break Pattern
// 
// All transports support circuit breaker:
// 
// ```mermaid
// stateDiagram-v2
//     [*] --> Closed
//     Closed --> Open: Threshold Exceeded
//     Open --> HalfOpen: Timeout
//     HalfOpen --> Closed: Success
//     HalfOpen --> Open: Failure
// ```
// 
// **Configurable Parameters:**
// 
// - Failure threshold: 5 errors
// - Success threshold: 2 successes to close
// - Open timeout: 60 seconds
// - Rolling window: 60 seconds
// 
// ### Monitoring
// 
// Unified metrics for all transports:
// 
// | Metric                               | Type      | Labels                               |
// | ------------------------------------ | --------- | ------------------------------------ |
// | `transport_request_duration_seconds` | Histogram | transport, method, status, component |
// | `transport_requests_total`           | Counter   | transport, method, status, peer      |
// | `transport_errors_total`             | Counter   | transport, error_code, method        |
// | `transport_active_connections`       | Gauge     | transport, peer                      |
// | `transport_circuit_breaker_state`    | Gauge     | transport                            |
// 
// ## Implementation Guidelines
// 
// ### Transport Implementation Checklist
// 
// When implementing a new transport:
// 
// - [ ] Implement `TransportStrategy` trait/interface
// - [ ] Add metrics collection
// - [ ] Implement error mapping to unified codes
// - [ ] Add retry logic with configurable backoff
// - [ ] Implement circuit breaker pattern
// - [ ] Add health check endpoints
// - [ ] Write comprehensive tests
// - [ ] Add documentation and examples
// 
// ### Adding a New Transport
// 
// 1. **Define transport-specific types**
// 2. **Implement TransportStrategy**
// 3. **Add metrics integration**
// 4. **Map errors to unified codes**
// 5. **Write tests**
// 6. **Update documentation**
// 
// ### Example: Adding a Custom Transport
// 
// ```rust
// // src/transport/my_transport.rs
// use crate::transport::{TransportStrategy, UnifiedRequest, UnifiedResponse, Notification, TransportError};
// use async_trait::async_trait;
// use prometheus::Histogram;
// 
// pub struct MyTransport {
//     connected: bool,
//     metrics_req_latency: Histogram,
// }
// 
// #[async_trait]
// impl TransportStrategy for MyTransport {
//     type Request = UnifiedRequest;
//     type Response = UnifiedResponse;
//     type Error = TransportError;
// 
//     async fn connect(&mut self) -> Result<(), Self::Error> {
//         // Connection logic
//         self.connected = true;
//         Ok(())
//     }
// 
//     async fn disconnect(&mut self) -> Result<(), Self::Error> {
//         self.connected = false;
//         Ok(())
//     }
// 
//     async fn send_request(&mut self, request: Self::Request) -> Result<Self::Response, Self::Error> {
//         let start = std::time::Instant::now();
// 
//         // Send and receive logic
//         let response = self.do_send(request).await?;
// 
//         // Record metrics
//         let duration = start.elapsed();
//         self.metrics_req_latency.observe(duration.as_secs_f64());
// 
//         Ok(response)
//     }
// 
//     async fn send_notification(&mut self, notification: Notification) -> Result<(), Self::Error> {
//         // Send notification logic
//         Ok(())
//     }
// 
//     async fn stream_events(&mut self) -> Result<BoxStream<'static, StreamEvent>, Self::Error> {
//         // Stream logic
//         Err(TransportError::Protocol("Streaming not supported".into()))
//     }
// 
//     fn is_connected(&self) -> bool {
//         self.connected
//     }
// 
//     fn latency_ms(&self) -> u64 {
//         // Calculate or return measured latency
//         10
//     }
// }
// ```
// 
// ### Transport Registry
// 
// Dynamic transport selection and management:
// 
// ```rust
// pub struct TransportRegistry {
//     transports: HashMap<String, Box<dyn TransportStrategy>>,
//     active: Option<String>,
// }
// 
// impl TransportRegistry {
//     pub fn register(&mut self, name: String, transport: Box<dyn TransportStrategy>) {
//         self.transports.insert(name, transport);
//     }
// 
//     pub fn select(&mut self, name: &str) -> Result<&mut dyn TransportStrategy, TransportError> {
//         self.transports.get_mut(name)
//             .map(|t| t.as_ref())
//             .map_or(Err(TransportError::Connection(format!("Transport {} not found", name))), Ok)
//     }
// 
//     pub fn get_active(&mut self) -> Option<&mut dyn TransportStrategy> {
//         self.active.as_ref()
//             .and_then(|name| self.transports.get_mut(name))
//             .map(|t| t.as_ref())
//     }
// }
// ```
// 
// ## Best Practices
// 
// ### Performance
// 
// 1. **Use Protocol Buffers** for best performance
// 2. **Enable compression** for payloads > 1KB
// 3. **Use connection pooling** where available
// 4. **Batch small requests** when possible
// 5. **Measure and profile** transport performance
// 
// ### Reliability
// 
// 1. **Implement retry** for transient errors
// 2. **Use circuit breaker** to prevent cascading failures
// 3. **Set appropriate timeouts** based on SLAs
// 4. **Monitor all metrics** and set alerts
// 5. **Test failure scenarios** in integration tests
// 
// ### Security
// 
// 1. **Use TLS** for network transports
// 2. **Validate all inputs** before processing
// 3. **Implement rate limiting** on server side
// 4. **Sanitize error messages** before returning to clients
// 5. **Use secure defaults** for all configurations
// 
// ### Debugging
// 
// 1. **Include correlation IDs** in all logs
// 2. **Capture request/response payloads** for debugging (when safe)
// 3. **Trace request lifecycles** end-to-end
// 4. **Use structured logging** with consistent fields
// 5. **Test with varying network conditions**
// 
// ## Testing
// 
// ### Unit Tests
// 
// Test individual transport methods in isolation:
// 
// ```rust
// #[cfg(test)]
// mod tests {
//     use super::*;
// 
//     #[tokio::test]
//     async fn test_send_request_success() {
//         let mut transport = MyTransport::new();
//         transport.connect().await.unwrap();
// 
//         let request = UnifiedRequest::new("test_method");
//         let response = transport.send_request(request).await;
// 
//         assert!(response.is_ok());
//     }
// 
//     #[tokio::test]
//     async fn test_retry_on_timeout() {
//         let mut transport = MyTransport::new();
//         let request = UnifiedRequest::new("test_method");
// 
//         let result = retry_with_backoff(&RetryConfig::default(), || {
//             transport.send_request(request.clone())
//         }).await;
// 
//         assert!(result.is_ok());
//     }
// }
// ```
// 
// ### Integration Tests
// 
// Test transports working together:
// 
// ```rust
// #[tokio::test]
// async fn test_transport_fallback() {
//     let primary = MyTransport::new(); // Simulate failing transport
//     let fallback = SecondTransport::new();
//     let mut fallback_transport = TransportFallback::new(primary, fallback);
// 
//     let request = UnifiedRequest::new("test_method");
//     let response = fallback_transport.send_request(request).await;
// 
//     assert!(response.is_ok());
// }
// ```
// 
// ### Load Tests
// 
// Test performance under high load:
// 
// ```bash
// # Using k6
// k6 run --vus 100 --duration 2m tests/load/transport_load.js
// ```
// 
// ## FAQ
// 
// ### Q: Which transport should I use?
// 
// **A:** Use the automatic selection logic unless you have specific requirements:
// 
// - **Desktop, same-process**: IPC (planned)
// - **Desktop, cross-process**: gRPC
// - **Web**: WASM (planned)
// - **Remote/Cross-network**: gRPC
// 
// ### Q: Can I use multiple transports simultaneously?
// 
// **A:** Yes, use the `TransportRegistry` to manage multiple transports and
// implement fallback logic with `TransportFallback`.
// 
// ### Q: How do I handle large payloads?
// 
// **A:** Use streaming APIs for large data, enable compression, and consider
// chunking for very large files (>100MB).
// 
// ### Q: What happens when a transport fails?
// 
// **A:** Depending on configuration, the transport will:
// 
// 1. Retry with exponential backoff (for retryable errors)
// 2. Circuit breaker trips after threshold (for repeated failures)
// 3. Fallback to alternative transport (if configured)
// 4. Return error to caller (if no fallback)
// 
// ### Q: How do I add custom metrics?
// 
// **A:** All transports expose metrics via Prometheus. Use
// `prometheus::Registry::register()` to add custom metrics.
// 
// ### Q: Is WASM supported in all browsers?
// 
// **A:** WASM has excellent browser support (Chrome 57+, Firefox 52+, Safari 11+,
// Edge 16+). For older browsers, use JSON over WebSocket.
// 
// ### Q: How do I debug transport issues?
// 
// **A:** Enable debug logging, check health endpoints, review metrics in Grafana,
// and use correlation IDs to trace requests.
// 
// ### Q: What's the overhead of the transport abstraction?
// 
// **A:** The abstraction layer adds <1% overhead compared to direct transport
// usage. Most overhead comes from serialization and networking.
// 
