// 
// **Test Plan**:
// 
// | Test Case                        | Expected Result         | Priority |
// | -------------------------------- | ----------------------- | -------- |
// | Workbench loads successfully     | No CSP errors           | P0       |
// | File explorer displays directory | Shows files             | P0       |
// | File can be opened               | Editor displays content | P0       |
// | File can be saved                | Changes persisted       | P0       |
// | Extension host spawns            | Cocoon process starts   | P1       |
// | Configuration loads              | Settings from Mountain  | P1       |
// | Terminal works                   | PTY functional          | P2       |
// 
// ### 8.4 Known Limitations
// 
// | Feature                        | Status           | Notes                              |
// | ------------------------------ | ---------------- | ---------------------------------- |
// | File system via vscode-file:// | ✅ Polyfilled    | Routes through Mountain            |
// | Extension host (Cocoon)        | ✅ Supported     | Via child process polyfill         |
// | Shared process services        | ⚠️ Partial       | Some services not available        |
// | Native modules                 | ❌ Not supported | Cannot load native Node.js modules |
// | Synchronous IPC                | ⚠️ Limited       | Tauri is async-only                |
// | Multiple windows               | ❌ Not supported | Electron multi-window model        |
// | Utility processes              | ❌ Not supported | Would require full process manager |
// 
// ### 8.5 CSP Resolution
// 
// The Electron workbench uses vscode-file:// protocol which violates CSP.
// Solutions:
// 
// **Option 1**: Modify CSP to allow file: scheme
// 
// ```html
// <meta
// 	content="default-src 'self' file:; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' 'unsafe-eval' file:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline';"
// 	http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy" />
// ```
// 
// **Option 2**: Use nonce-based CSP
// 
// ```html
// <script nonce="<%= nonce %>" src="vscode-file://..."></script>
// ```
// 
// **Option 3**: Runtime CSP override (Tauri configuration)
// 
// ```json
// {
// 	"tauri": {
// 		"security": {
// 			"csp": "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' 'unsafe-eval';"
// 		}
// 	}
// }
// ```
// 
// **Recommendation**: Use Option 3 in Tauri config for development, tighten for
// production.
// 
// ---
// 
// ## 9. Recommendations
// 
// ### 9.1 Primary Recommendation: A2 - Medium-Friction Approach
// 
// **Rationale**:
// 
// 1. **Best Risk/Reward Balance**: A2 offers 80-90% of VSCode Electron workbench
//    functionality with moderate implementation effort.
// 
// 2. **Leverages Existing Infrastructure**:
//     - Browser workbench is already web-compatible
//     - Mountain IPC handlers are already implemented
//     - Wind services are partially complete
//     - Cocoon extension host is functional
// 
// 3. **Maintainability**:
//     - Uses upstream VSCode code (browser workbench)
//     - Custom providers are well-isolated
//     - Clear separation of concerns
// 
// 4. **Performance**:
//     - Direct Tauri IPC (no remote agent overhead)
//     - Native file system access through Mountain
//     - No protocol interception overhead
// 
// 5. **Future-Proof**:
//     - Can transition to A1 or A3 as needed
//     - Modular architecture supports evolution
//     - Extensible for new features
// 
// ### 9.2 Secondary Recommendation: A1 - Low-Friction Approach
// 
// **When to Use**:
// 
// - Need quick proof-of-concept
// - Time constraints (2-3 weeks)
// - Basic editing functionality sufficient
// - Can accept some performance overhead
// 
// **Use Cases**:
// 
// - Initial Tauri/Mountain integration testing
// - Development environment
// - Feature exploration
// 
// ### 9.3 Tertiary Recommendation: A4 - Native Wind Workbench
// 
// **When to Use**:
// 
// - Need complete control over architecture
// - VSCode Electron workbench constraints unacceptable
// - Long-term custom development capacity
// - Willing to build missing features from scratch
// 
// **Use Cases**:
// 
// - Specialized editor requirements
// - Experimental features
// - Education/research
// 
// ### 9.4 Not Recommended: A3 - High-Frigration Approach
// 
// **Rationale**:
// 
// 1. **High Maintenance Burden**:
//     - Constant battle with Electron API changes
//     - Polyfills break frequently
//     - Difficult to debug
// 
// 2. **Limited Benefit**:
//     - Browser workbench offers 80% of functionality
//     - Most features don't need native access
//     - Complexity outweighs benefits
// 
// 3. **Security Concerns**:
//     - CSP violations
//     - Polyfill attack surface
//     - Electron-specific vulnerabilities
// 
// 4. **Performance Issues**:
//     - Protocol interception overhead
//     - Polyfill indirection
//     - Synchronous IPC emulation
// 
// **When A3 Might Be Considered**:
// 
// - Must support existing Electron extensions that depend 100% on native APIs
// - Legal/compliance requirements demand exact Electron parity
// - Unlimited development resources available
// 
